  "client_view_text": " Here's a write-ahead log (WAL) file for a simple database:\n````sql\n 00000001   00000000   00000000   00000000   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000\n00000000   00000000   00000000   00000000   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000\n00000000   00000000   00000000   00000000   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000\n000000 To explain this concept clear and concise, use an example.\nLet's say we have a simple database with two tables, `users` and `users_other_data`. We can insert data into the tables, retrieve data, and even make some queries to see how data behaves in the database.\n\n```sql\n-- Table `users`\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    user_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(255)\n);\n\n-- Table `users_other_data`\nCREATE TABLE users_other_data (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    user_id INT,\n    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(user_id)\n);\n\n-- Insert some test data\nINSERT INTO users (user_id, name) VALUES (1, 'John Doe');\nINSERT INTO users (user_id, name) VALUES (2, 'Jane Doe');\n\n-- Insert some test data into `users_other_data`\nINSERT INTO users_other_data (id, user_id) VALUES (1, 1);\nINSERT INTO users_other_data (id, user_id) VALUES (2, 2);\n\n-- Query the database\nSELECT * FROM users;\nSELECT * FROM users_other_data;\n```\n\nWhen we run these SQL queries, the database writes the resulting data to the disk. However, after a crash, the database may not be able"
